# The BIP39 wordlist is verified at load time against a SHA-256 constant
# (Bip39.WORDLIST_SHA256), so it has to arrive byte-for-byte identical on every
# platform. Without this, a checkout on Windows rewrites its 2048 line endings
# to CRLF, the file grows from 13116 to 15164 bytes, the digest no longer
# matches, and WordList.load throws CorruptWordlist -- an app that can neither
# create nor restore a wallet, from a clean clone, with no local change to blame.
#
# `-text` means never convert in either direction, which is what a digest-checked
# file needs. Marking it `text eol=lf` would be weaker: that still normalises on
# commit and only fixes the checkout side.
contrib/android/app/src/main/assets/bip39/english.txt -text

# Same reasoning, lower stakes: golden vectors are compared literally.
contrib/android/app/src/test/resources/qr_vectors.txt -text

# Anything that RUNS on Linux keeps LF whatever the checkout platform does.
# Not cosmetic: build-deb.sh copies these scripts verbatim into the .deb, and a
# shebang arriving as "#!/bin/sh
" fails with a bare "No such file or
# directory" that names the interpreter rather than the carriage return -- one
# of the least obvious errors in packaging. The extensionless three are the
# wizard, the live view and the miner supervisor.
*.sh                                      text eol=lf
contrib/linux-deb/pcoin-setup             text eol=lf
contrib/linux-deb/pcoin-mine              text eol=lf
contrib/linux-deb/pcoin-miner-supervisor  text eol=lf
# contrib/seed-monitoring is the root-owned monitoring that every host runs,
# and this list used to name its scripts one at a time -- nine of them, across
# two passes, each added only after the gap was rediscovered. Audited
# 2026-10-24: 38 files in the directory carry a shebang and run on Linux, and
# 29 of those still had NO rule at all -- including pcoin-facts,
# pcoin-group-watch, pcoin-deposit-watch, pcoin-approve and pcoin-unit-failed,
# which are the live services rather than the stragglers. A Windows checkout
# rewrites those to CRLF and the kernel then refuses to exec them, reporting
# `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`: an error that names the interpreter and not
# the stray return, so it surfaces at a restart and not at a deploy. Nothing in
# the directory is binary (checked before this), so pin the whole of it and let
# the next addition inherit the rule instead of rediscovering this a tenth
# time. Same decision, and the same reasoning, as contrib/backup below.
contrib/seed-monitoring/*             text eol=lf
# contrib/wpcn's two extensionless scripts had NO rule at all -- an omission,
# not a decision. Both carry a shebang and run on a Linux box, and git was
# reporting "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" on the
# keeper, so a fresh clone would have produced a file the kernel refuses to
# exec. Same gap as the four above, found the same way.
contrib/wpcn/pcoin-wpcn-keeper   text eol=lf
contrib/wpcn/pcoin-redeem-watch  text eol=lf
contrib/wpcn/pcoin-price-watch   text eol=lf
# systemd tolerates CRLF, but there is no reason to ship it to a Linux box.
*.service  text eol=lf
*.timer    text eol=lf
# pcoin-seed-vault.mjs carries a shebang and is meant to be runnable on a vault
# host (`verify` is the backup check), so it inherits the same reasoning as the
# .deb scripts above: a CRLF checkout turns `./pcoin-seed-vault.mjs` into "No
# such file or directory" naming the interpreter rather than the stray return.
*.mjs      text eol=lf

# Windows-only sources keep CRLF, which is what the in-box csc and Inno Setup
# expect to see.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.iss  text eol=crlf

# contrib/ops holds root-owned scripts for the payments host. Two of them carry
# a `#!/bin/bash` shebang and no extension, so none of the rules above reach
# them — a Windows checkout would rewrite them to CRLF and the kernel would then
# refuse to run them, reporting `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`: an error that
# names the interpreter rather than the stray carriage return, on a file that
# looks perfectly correct in every editor. These are the caddy watchdog and the
# Cloudflare firewall refresher, so the failure would be a host with no watchdog
# and a stale allowlist, discovered later rather than at deploy time.
contrib/ops/pcoin-caddy-watchdog  text eol=lf
contrib/ops/pcoin-cf-firewall     text eol=lf
contrib/ops/*.conf                text eol=lf

# contrib/backup had no rule at all -- the same omission as the seed-monitoring
# four and the wpcn three, and this time it was not merely latent. Deploying
# contrib/backup/pcoin-backup-pull from a Windows worktree produced
# `syntax error near unexpected token $'{\r''` on the backup host, caught only
# because a `bash -n` gate ran before any live file was replaced. The directory
# is root-owned backup machinery on the vault host, so a backup that cannot
# start is a backup that does not exist; pin the whole directory rather than
# the two names in it, so the next addition inherits the rule instead of
# repeating the discovery.
contrib/backup/*                  text eol=lf

# The Move PCN page's crypto bundle is pinned by SHA-256 in
# contrib/admin-panel/transfer.mjs and loaded with Subresource Integrity, so it
# has to arrive byte-for-byte identical on every platform -- the same reasoning
# as the BIP39 wordlist at the top of this file. A Windows checkout rewriting
# its line endings would make the panel refuse to serve it.
contrib/admin-panel/transfer-crypto.bundle.js   -text
# Its two companion scripts and the example config: LF everywhere, like *.mjs.
contrib/admin-panel/transfer-core.js            text eol=lf
contrib/admin-panel/transfer-ui.js              text eol=lf
contrib/admin-panel/transfer-destinations.example.json  text eol=lf
